<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · China, Tibet and Mongolia</p><h1 class="st-title">State of Xi</h1><p class="st-meta">1046 BCE – 680 BCE (approx.) · capital Xi</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A small Ji-clan state on the Huai whose fall around 680 BCE is tied to the story of Lady Xi, the silent queen taken by King Wen of Chu.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Xi was a minor Zhou state ruled by the Ji clan, located near modern Xixian in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henan">Henan</a>, on the northern edge of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huai-river">Huai River</a> plain. It lay close to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/state-of-cai">State of Cai</a> and within reach of the expanding <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/state-of-chu">State of Chu</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Little is recorded about its government. Xi appears in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zuo-zhuan">Zuo zhuan</a> chiefly in a story of feuding: the Marquis of Xi, angered that the Marquis of Cai had insulted his wife, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xi-gui">Xi Gui</a>, invited Chu to attack Cai, setting off a chain of events that drew Chu into the region.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/king-wen-of-chu">King Wen of Chu</a> destroyed Xi around 680 BCE and took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xi-gui">Xi Gui</a> as his wife; she bore the future <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/king-cheng-of-chu">King Cheng</a> but, by tradition, refused to speak to her new husband. Xi became a Chu county, an early example of Chu's administrative districts.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="kingdom, 1046 BCE – 680 BCE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M22 72 V40 L36 54 L50 28 L64 54 L78 40 V72 Z"/><path d="M22 80 H78"/><circle cx="50" cy="24" r="3"/><circle cx="22" cy="36" r="2.5"/><circle cx="78" cy="36" r="2.5"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">kingdom</p><p class="st-emblem-region">China, Tibet and Mongolia</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:38.89%;width:7.28%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1046 BCE – 680 BCE (approx.)</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Xi</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Zhou ancestral religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Old Chinese</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>China</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Xi (Zhou state)</dd></dl></aside></div>

